# Day 2: Learned Constructors and Object Initialization in C++

Today I learned about Constructors in C++ and how they are used to initialize objects automatically when they are created.

## What I Learned

### Constructor
A constructor is a special member function of a class that is called automatically when an object is created.

### Features of Constructors

- Constructor name is the same as the class name.
- Constructors do not have a return type.
- They are called automatically when an object is created.
- Used to initialize object data.

## Example Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;

    Student() {
        name = "Payal";
        age = 20;
    }

    void display() {
        cout << "Name: " << name << endl;
        cout << "Age: " << age << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s1;
    s1.display();

    return 0;
}
```

## Output

```
Name: Payal
Age: 20
```

## Types of Constructors

### 1. Default Constructor

```cpp
Student() {
    name = "Unknown";
    age = 0;
}
```

### 2. Parameterized Constructor

```cpp
Student(string n, int a) {
    name = n;
    age = a;
}
```

### Example

```cpp
Student s1("Payal", 20);
```

## Key Points

- Constructors are used to initialize objects.
- They are called automatically.
- A class can have multiple constructors.
- Constructor overloading is supported in C++.

## My Understanding

Constructors help initialize object values at the time of object creation. They reduce the need to assign values separately and make code more organized and readable.

